{"id":114432,"title":"Mathematical models for volume rendering and neutron transport","url":"https://digital.library.unt.edu/ark:/67531/metadc1274949/?q=volume rendering equation","final_url":"https://digital.library.unt.edu/ark:/67531/metadc1274949/m2/1/high_res_d/10116963.pdf","year":null,"kind":"link","source":"os:digital.library.unt.edu","doi":null,"arxiv":null,"sha256":"51efb4cd9b44b2a41c74e65291e79cd58032e2c98a25f35e1fe774cb932a0eca","ctype":"application/pdf","text_src":"url","fetched_at":1793236112.951278,"text_chars":17156,"abstract":"This paper reviews several different models for light interaction with volume densities of absorbing, glowing, reflecting, or scattering material. They include absorption only, glow only, glow and absorption combined, single scattering of external illumination, and multiple scattering. The models are derived from differential equations, and illustrated on a data set representing a cloud. They are related to corresponding models in neutron transport. The multiple scattering model uses an efficient method to propagate the radiation which does not suffer from the ray effect.","file":null,"marks":["unofficial"],"links":["https://digital.library.unt.edu/ark:/67531/metadc1274949/m2/1/high_res_d/10116963.pdf","https://digital.library.unt.edu/ark:/67531/metadc1274949/?q=volume rendering equation","https://digital.library.unt.edu/ark:/67531/metadc1274949/?q=volume illumination","https://digital.library.unt.edu/ark:/67531/metadc1274949/?q=phase function rendering","https://digital.library.unt.edu/ark:/67531/metadc1274949/?q=volume rendering integral","https://digital.library.unt.edu/ark:/67531/metadc1274949/?q=gradient shading volume rendering","https://digital.library.unt.edu/ark:/67531/metadc1274949/?q=max optical models","https://digital.library.unt.edu/ark:/67531/metadc1274949/?q=multiple scattering rendering","https://digital.library.unt.edu/ark:/67531/metadc1274949/?q=volume rendering integral approximation","https://digital.librar